{"id":"894955b7-574c-4e7c-963b-06b2a6659e03","object":"chat.completion","created":1785037426,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    # basic validation\\n    if \\\"root\\\" not in node_map:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing root node\\\")\\n    all_symbols = {n[\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in nodes}\\n    for s in required:\\n        if s not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{s}' not present\\\")\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n    retained = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n    removed = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if not n[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == \\\"root\\\":\\n            continue\\n        if n[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required:\\n            continue\\n        # check for retained children\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        # check required symbol uniqueness if this node's symbol is required\\n        if n[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required:\\n            count = sum(1 for rid in retained if node_map[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"] == n[\\\"symbol\\\"])\\n            if count == 1:\\n                continue\\n        # removal\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the given specification. The task is \"SE-PE-01\" which appears to be about grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. The payload contains nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list.\n\nThe goal: reduce the parse tree by removing removable nodes (with some constraints) while preserving that all required_symbols remain represented. The output must have removed_node_ids (list of IDs of removed nodes in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (list of IDs of retained nodes, sorted). The instructions say \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed.\n\nWe need to devise an algorithm. The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\"\n\nSo we need to process nodes in order: first by depth descending (deeper first), then by whatever tie-break? It says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for nodes at same depth, we should sort by ID. But the removal order should be the order we actually remove them. So we iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending, then ID ascending. For each node that is removable (and not root, not a required symbol? Actually root is not removable anyway, but required symbols might be removable? The spec says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we must not remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols. Also, if the node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove it because that would orphan children. So we need to track which nodes are retained/removed.\n\nWe also need to ensure that after tentatively removing a node, all required symbols remain represented. That means for each required symbol, there must be at least one node in the tree that has that symbol and is not removed. So we need to check that before removal.\n\nWe also need to consider \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\"? The description mentions \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" but we don't have a grammar. The payload only has nodes and required_symbols. Probably the grammar is implicit: the tree structure itself is valid. Removal is allowed for removable nodes if constraints satisfied.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n1. Build a map from node id to node data.\n2. Build a map from node id to list of children ids (by scanning nodes, for each node, if parent not null, add child to parent's children list).\n3. Initialize retained set with all node ids. removed list empty.\n4. Determine list of nodes sorted: first by depth descending, then by id ascending (string comparison? ID are like \"root\",\"n1\",\"n2\"... probably string compare lexicographically. That matches stable tie break.)\n5. For each node in that sorted list:\n   - Skip if not removable.\n   - Skip if node id == \"root\" (root is never removable, but data has removable: false so we can skip based on removable field).\n   - Skip if node's symbol is in required_symbols.\n   - Check if node has any children that are still retained (i.e., in retained set). If yes, skip (cannot remove because children would be orphaned).\n   - Tentatively remove: compute what symbols would be removed? Actually, removing a node means removing that node itself. The required_symbols must still be present elsewhere. So we need to check if after removal, every required symbol still has at least one node in retained set (excluding this node). So for each required symbol, count nodes with that symbol that are in retained set (including this node). If after removing this node, any required symbol count becomes 0, skip.\n   - If all checks pass, remove: remove node id from retained set, append node id to removed list in order.\n6. After processing all nodes, retained_node_ids = sorted list of retained set (sorted by id? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". Sorting by ID is natural. Probably ascending string order.)\n7. removed_node_ids = the list in removal order.\n\nOne nuance: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That is checked. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That covers the required symbols check.\n\nAlso note: The spec says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So depth descending first. That is typical for Perses: first try to remove deeper nodes.\n\nTie break: use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth. So we sort by depth descending, then ID ascending.\n\nNow, should we consider that after removing a node, its ancestors might become removable? The process is a single pass: we iterate once over sorted list. That should be fine because if a node's children are removed later (but we process deeper nodes first), the parent might already have been considered before its children are removed? Actually we process deeper nodes first. So when we process a parent at depth d, its children (depth d+1) have already been processed (since deeper first). At that point, children might have been removed or retained. If all children are removed, then the parent might have no retained children, so it could be considered for removal later? But we already processed parent before children? Wait: deeper first means depth d+1 before depth d. So when we reach depth d, its children at depth d+1 have already been processed, possibly removed. So in our iteration, we will have already processed children. So the parent's retained children set is accurate. Good.\n\nHowever, a parent might become removable only after its children are removed. Since we process parent after children, that is correct: we will evaluate parent after children have been removed. But note: we might have already skipped the parent earlier? No, we process each node exactly once in order. So the parent will be considered after its children. That's fine.\n\nIf a node at depth d is removable and all its children are removed, and its symbol is not required, and removing it doesn't remove the last instance of a required symbol, then we can remove it. That's correct.\n\nThus the algorithm works.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- case is a dict with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Also maybe \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n- nodes list of dicts with keys: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. utility is given but not used in algorithm? It might be used elsewhere but spec says \"Use only quantities present in the case payload\". But the description doesn't mention utility for decision. It does mention \"registered_candidate_order\" but we are not given candidate order. Probably we ignore utility.\n- We must validate: raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. What constitutes invalid? If required_symbols includes a symbol that doesn't appear in any node? Then it's infeasible? The problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a required symbol is missing initially, that's infeasible. Also if root is missing or not removable? But root is given. We can check that root exists and is not removable. Also check that all nodes have required fields. If any violation, raise ValueError.\n\nSimplify: assume well-formed input, but add basic checks.\n\nNow produce Python code for solve(case). Must not mutate case.\n\nReturn a dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nEdge: The returned list for retained_node_ids should be sorted. The removed_node_ids should be in removal order.\n\nNow produce implementation source as a string inside JSON with key \"implementation\". The whole response must be a JSON object with one key \"implementation\" whose value is a string containing Python code.\n\nWe need to escape the string properly for JSON.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n[\"parent\"]\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Validate: root exists and not removable? Already.\n    # Validate required symbols present\n    all_symbols = set(n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes)\n    for s in required:\n        if s not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(\"Required symbol '{}' not in tree\".format(s))\n    # Sorted nodes for processing\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if nid == \"root\":  # safety\n            continue\n        if n[\"symbol\"] in required:\n            continue\n        # check if any child retained\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # check required symbols after removal\n        # Compute counts: we could do quick check for each required symbol if it only appears in this node\n        # But more robust: for each req symbol, count nodes with that symbol in retained (excluding this node)\n        # If any count becomes 0, skip.\n        req_ok = True\n        for s in required:\n            count = sum(1 for nid2 in retained if node_map[nid2][\"symbol\"] == s)\n            # if this node's symbol is s, then after removal count will be count-1\n            if n[\"symbol\"] == s:\n                if count <= 1:\n                    req_ok = False\n                    break\n            else:\n                # not affected\n                pass\n        if not req_ok:\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    # retained_node_ids sorted\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nCheck: The required symbol check above: if the node's symbol is not required, then removal does not affect count of required symbols. So only need to check if node's symbol is required? But required symbols must remain represented. If node's symbol is not required, its removal does not affect required symbols. However, there is a scenario where a node with non-required symbol might be the only parent of nodes that contain required symbols? No, required symbols are about the symbols themselves, not about tree structure. The spec says \"all required symbols remain represented\" - meaning at least one node with that symbol must still be present. So only nodes whose symbol is in required set can affect the count. So we can simplify: only check if node's symbol is required and if after removal there is no other node with that symbol. But careful: what if the node has a required symbol but there are other nodes with same symbol? Then removal is ok. So the check is: if n[\"symbol\"] in required and sum(1 for nid2 in retained if node_map[nid2][\"symbol\"] == n[\"symbol\"]) == 1: then skip. That's sufficient because only that symbol could become missing.\n\nBut also note: required symbols might be multiple distinct symbols. So we only need to check that specific symbol if the node's symbol is in required set. So better:\n\nif n[\"symbol\"] in required:\n    count = sum(1 for rid in retained if node_map[rid][\"symbol\"] == n[\"symbol\"])\n    if count == 1:  # this node is the only one\n        continue\n\nThat's simpler. But we need to ensure that we don't miss case where node's symbol is not required but removal somehow causes a required symbol to disappear? Not possible because removal only removes this node; it doesn't affect other nodes' symbols. So fine.\n\nThus final implementation.\n\nAlso we must ensure we don't mutate input. We are using case data to build maps, but not modifying case.\n\nNow produce the JSON response with implementation.\n\nWe must escape the Python code as a JSON string. Use triple quotes? No, we need to write a proper JSON string with newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". We'll write the code as a multiline string and then escape it manually or we can rely on Python's json.dumps to do it for us, but we are writing the response manually. Better to write the code and then escape. Since this is a text response, we can construct the JSON object with the implementation string. I'll write the Python code in a readable format, then escape all necessary characters.\n\nI'll produce a JSON object like:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\\n\"}\n\nWe need to ensure there are no Markdown fences. Just the raw JSON.\n\nLet's write the code carefully, then escape.\n\nI'll include docstring maybe not needed. Keep minimal.\n\nAlso raise ValueError for invalid. We'll add check for root existence and required symbols existence.\n\nNow write final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3273,"total_tokens":5445,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2820},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}